# Build accessible interfaces from semantic tokens and shared primitives

# Frontend styling

Use the generated component primitives and semantic design tokens. Theme intent such as primary,
muted, destructive, border, surface, and foreground must not be replaced with arbitrary fixed colors
or duplicated component CSS. Extend tokens centrally before repeating a new visual literal.

Compose pages from shared layout, header, card/section, form, table/list, loading, empty, error, and
notification patterns. Keep width, spacing, type scale, and information density consistent with the
nearest shipped surface. Support narrow and wide viewports; avoid layouts that depend on a fixed
screen size or pixel position.

Prefer native semantic elements. Every interaction has a visible hover/focus/disabled state, an
accessible name, keyboard behavior, and a clear pointer affordance. A non-native clickable element
must provide the equivalent role, focusability, and Enter/Space handling; use a button or link when
possible. A mouse-clickable table row also exposes a keyboard-reachable primary action.

Never require an operator to type an internal database identity. Use a searchable, labeled selection
surface for relationships. Critical setup flows must explain prerequisites and disabled states rather
than presenting an inert control.

Accessibility-tree assertions protect semantics and state. Screenshots are reserved for visual
properties such as layout, theme, and responsive behavior.
